the legend will be born welcome to  planet arrakis the neville news dune is  one of the biggest films of 2021 and  quite a bit went into making it from  practical effects like moving platforms  and otherworldly sets it's amazing to  watch it director de nivel new didn't  want to rely on green screen and cg when  bringing the planet of arrakis to the  big screen villeneuve insisted that the  film be shot in the jordanian desert of  wadi rum which is known as the valley of  the moon they didn't shoot jaws in a  swimming pool villeneuve wanted to use  as many real environments as possible  and wanted the cast and crew behind the  film to be inspired by infinity what may  surprise fans is that according to  chalamet i think it's literally two  scenes i did on a green screen and uh  besides that everything else was  practical the filming conditions in the  jordanian desert of wadi rum weren't the  most preferable for the actors in front  of the cameras it gets so hot during the  day that sometimes you have to shoot  between 5am and 7am and 5pm and 7pm i  guess it'd be too dangerous to be  shooting outside filming also took place  in abu dhabi's liwa desert which was the  same desert that star wars the force  awakens filmed making it a bit of a  homecoming for oscar isaac the tents on  set had to be treated with air  conditioning as the movie was being  filmed on location in july and august  which are the hottest months of the year  in the liwa desert  i can't walk like regular humans the  team behind dune took a page from how  classical blockbusters were made when it  came to the film's practical effects  paul lambert the two-time oscar-winning  vfx supervisor behind dune revealed that  a four meter wide platform was made and  mounted on a system that allowed it to  vibrate at different speeds as soon as  that vibrating starts audiences will be  able to notice the actor's feet sinking  into the sand  when creating out-of-this-world vehicles  most films would either resort to cgi or  small-scale models that wasn't the case  when it came time to bring dunes iconic  thopters to life on the big screen the  thopters were made using gimbals on the  high hills of budapest the gimbals were  used so that the crew could raise lower  rotate and pivot the thopter on an axis  this would help make the thopter scenes  feel more real and even more intense for  audience members  they fight like demons an epic like dune  needs to have some intricate fight  scenes and denis villeneuve knew just  the guy to choreograph the fight scenes  roger yuan juan worked as a fight  coordinator for several major action  franchises such as the bourne films and  the james bond film skyfall with dune he  knew that each group of characters would  need their own fighting style for the  atreides joanne used more of a filipino  style of kali and escrima for the  sardakar yuan based the fighting moves  up for the ways of vikings and samurai  for the villainous and brutal harkenings  yuan decided to give them a more  beastial and sadistic fighting style  you fight when the necessity arises no  matter the mood while much of the  exterior shots of dune were filmed in  the jordanian desert of wadi rum for the  more detailed interiors real sets were  built inside studios in budapest one of  the main sets that was built in budapest  was the emperor's palace on arrakis the  sets were built as massive environments  and rooms so that their physical reality  could give the actors and actresses  ideas so they could each bring something  different into their performances the  massive size of the sets impressed even  oscar isaac it really made me feel like  i was in an alien planet from the ever  expansive architecture to the vast  desert and the larger-than-life ship the  physical sets were unlike anything most  of the cast had ever seen before  the visuals  the lighting  craig's unbelievable since dune takes  place on a different planet it'd be  strange if all the inhabitants just  spoke english which means that the team  behind the film would have to create a  new language the creative team members  contacted the king kongling david  peterson who worked on creating  languages for game of thrones to help  build languages for the film peterson  worked on four different linguistic  systems for dune two were spoken and two  were signed and also worked on an entire  writing system  this is doctor uh  the designs of the interiors of arrakis  were made to look as authentic as  possible in fact the crew behind the  film implemented cultural links from the  story into the carvings on the walls  this includes the sand worms which are  one of the most recognizable icons from  the dune lore one of the frescoes  showing off a carving of the sand worms  was made to look like it was carved by  the fremen working for the governor  greg fraser who served as the  cinematographer on the film found  himself in a bit of a challenge when  filming the interior scenes of the  arrakis compound the compound had no  windows as thematically it's supposed to  withstand war and heat production  designer patrice vermette and denis  villeneuve solved this by using light  wells throughout the fortress set the  light is effectively the byproduct of  bounce light that comes in through the  shaft it's not direct harsh sunlight  that's true like many other modern  blockbusters dune used the imax format  for entire sections of the film which  includes paul's vision as well as the  dream and desert sequences the rest of  the film was shot in the traditional 235  format which is used for most modern  movies in order to maintain the impact  and awe of the imax on the audience  villeneuve claimed that it was  beneficial to step away from time to  time villeneuve also made the decision  to film the desert scenes with a  handheld camera but the rest of the  world in the film would be more similar  to tableau  stellen skarsgard stars as dunes robust  villain baron harkin and claims that his  time in the makeup chair made up 30  percent of his entire time working on  the movie when it came time to film  baron's black murky bath the crew  debated about doing the scene digitally  skarsgard wasn't too bothered at that  point and didn't want to get out of his  intricate suit so he happily dipped  himself into that oh so unappealing bath  i got one surprise to fall color was  another major component that went into  making dune the neville nouve wanted  arakis to be harsh and desolate and an  environment that would be unwelcoming to  outsiders villeneuve along with the  cinematographer and production designers  tried their best to never make the sky  in the film look blue a lot of desert  photography was also done where there  was both yellow sand and a blue sky but  to avoid this the team veered away from  that and decided to make the sand look  more washed out and the sky more white  han zimmer is one of the most legendary  and influential composers in the  business zimmer had even turned down  working with christopher nolan so that  he could work on dune one of the most  interesting aspects of the dune score is  the use of bagpipes which according to  zimmer was completely denivel nouve's  idea after noticing a bagpiper in a  scene in the movie zimmer turned to  villeneuve who said well you can't just  do trumpets you can't do fanfare but  this ancient instrument could have been  around forever from there zimmer ended  up recruiting 30 different bagpipers to  make up for the unconventional addition  to the score production designer patrice  vermette had some unconventional ways of  finding locations to stand in for  arrakis in fact vermette used google  earth to scour the globe for potential  filming locations before settling on  wadi rum including iran chad mauritania  and libya vermette and villeneuve  selected wadi rum as they found it to be  like a trade show of rock formations  the pandemic caused some problems during  the post-production process of dune as  director denis villeneuve was unable to  work in the same room as his long-term  editor joe walker villeneuve claimed  that working separately was not the same  and that editing was much like playing  music you need to feel the other feel  his reaction feel your own reaction  there are so many ideas that joe and i  have i don't know if it's his idea or my  idea it comes from the addition of us  both being in the room which is by far  my favorite thing about cinema despite  dune being a passion project for denis  vlnew there have been plenty of other  attempts to bring frank herbert's novel  to the screen outside of the david lynch  movie from 1984 legendary filmmaker  alejandro yodorowski attempted to bring  it to life but to no avail years later  lone survivor filmmaker peter berg  attempted to bring the series to life  but was unsuccessful it wasn't until  denis villeneuve signed on to bring  frank herbert's vision to life that  things finally came together how dare  you use the voice on me from very  intricate details made with the sets  costumes and practical effects director  denis villeneuve and his team were  clearly passionate about making dune and  it truly shows on screen are you excited  to check out dune
 

